# Target C: one-deficit completion or a bounded Delaunay defect set

Date: 2026-10-07. Authority: audit.75, main
`baadff26b3fae1107f8ed2d9d18f03f78cd4a23b`.
**Proved candidate awaiting independent audit.** No promotion.

## 1. Exact one-deficit alternative

Let F be a convex unit wall with b vertices and z old interior points,
all mutually unit-separated. Put a=sqrt(3)/4, a_0=2*a and

`sigma_0=area(F)-a*(2*z+b-2)`.

Assume 0<=sigma_0<=a_0+1/1000. Extend the packing to a saturated
auxiliary plane packing as in the effective small-slack report. At most
one point is added in F. Exactly one of the following occurs:

1. One point is added. The completed cloud has area-form slack at most
   1/1000 and has the complete developed triangular-lattice polygon
   chart of that report. The old cloud is that *controlled deformed*
   chart with the single added point omitted.
2. No point is added. The old cloud is saturated in F and has a
   Delaunay triangulation with at most 51 obtuse triangles and at most
   541 exceptional vertices. Outside this bounded set all incident
   triangles are acute with individual area excess below 1/100, and
   every interior vertex has six incident triangles.

This is independent of M and of the cloud's initial coordinates. It does
not assert the old points form an exact lattice with a vacancy. In the
second branch the defect set can obstruct global equilateral development;
no vacancy relocation or packet is supplied by its cardinality alone.
The metric excess need not localize in that exceptional set. The exact
macroscopic rhombic-grid regression below has one area-unit of slack,
no extra insertable site, and no exceptional vertex for M>=7: its metric
strain is spread over all cells. A controlled metric budget remains
essential even when the combinatorial defect count is zero.

For the arithmetic six-wall z=N-1 cloud at
0<theta<=1/(10000*M^3), the full-count area bound gives this hypothesis.
The new packet already excludes that specified one-deficit wall class.
This theorem instead makes an arbitrary near-equality unit-wall cloud
available to future wall extraction/defect movement without relying on
the unproved strong-solidity classification.

## 2. Completion accounting is exact

Oler applied to the b wall labels and the z interior labels gives
sigma_0>=0. Each added interior point increases the triangle count
2*z+b-2 by two. If k points are added, the completed area slack is

`sigma=sigma_0-a_0*k>=0`.

Therefore k<=floor(sigma_0/a_0). Under the hypothesis, k is zero or
one. No new point can lie in the relative interior of a unit wall edge,
by separation from its endpoints, so every added point of F is interior.
If k=1, its residual slack is between zero and 1/1000. Apply the proved
candidate effective-chart theorem at that exact scope.

The additions are auxiliary. They do not disappear from a source ledger
and are not a same-cardinality competitor. If one later uses them in a
finite source replacement, their full count, contacts, triangles and
upper constraints must be paid and checked separately.

## 3. Defects charged to a single nonoverlapping area currency

The saturated triangulation has sides at least one and circumradii at
most one. Every triangle has area at least a, and
`sum_T sigma_T=sigma`, where sigma_T=area(T)-a>=0.
These are disjoint triangle areas; this is not a sum of overlapping
nested stress demands.

Let H be the number of obtuse-or-right triangles. For each such triangle,
its longest edge is internal and the opposite neighbour gives the pair
gap

`sigma_T+sigma_neighbour >= delta=(2-sqrt(3))/4`.

This exact gap is proved in the effective-chart report from the local
Delaunay angle inequality and the unit-side/circumradius bounds. Summing
over obtuse triangles charges each triangle at most four times: once
as itself, and at most once across each of its three edges as a neighbour.
Thus

`H*delta<=4*sigma`.

At sigma<=a_0+1/1000 one has 4*sigma<52*delta, so H<=51.
This is an exact universal bound, not negative computational search.

There are at most floor(100*sigma)<=86 triangles with sigma_T>=1/100.
Call these and the obtuse triangles defective. Their union has at most
137 triangles and at most 411 incident vertices. Every remaining triangle
is acute, has side lengths below 11/10, and satisfies

`max_side-1<=5*sigma_T`,
`|angle-pi/3|<=50*sigma_T`.

The side/angle proof only uses sigma_T<1/100; the threshold is below
f(11/10)-a and below 1/2-a, as required by that proof.

At an interior vertex incident to no defective triangle, valence k!=6
forces the total incident area excess S_v to satisfy

`50*S_v >= |2*pi-k*pi/3| >= pi/3`,

so S_v>1/50. Across all such vertices, sum S_v<=3*sigma. There are
therefore fewer than 150*sigma<131 such vertices, hence at most 130.
Together with the vertices incident to defective triangles this gives
at most 411+130=541 exceptional vertices.

No position, diameter incidence, contact degree or stress multiplier is
silently inferred from being exceptional. The conclusion is a finite
Delaunay combinatorial/metric defect budget.

## 4. A general boundary-order completion theorem

The same proof works for arbitrary finite nonnegative sigma_0. It gives
a completed saturated cloud with

`k<=sigma_0/a_0` auxiliary additions,
`H<=4*sigma/delta`,
`number of exceptional vertices <= (12/delta+450)*sigma`,

with integer rounding downward where appropriate. Here sigma is the
residual slack after the additions. The exceptional set includes vertices
of triangles with excess >=1/100, all obtuse triangles, and all remaining
interior vertices of valence other than six. Constants do not depend on
cardinality, diameter or shape.

Consequently an actual bounded inactive **unit-contact face** in the
canonical macroscopic branch, with perimeter b=O(D) and area/population
agreement to O(D), admits an auxiliary completion costing O(D) sites
and a Delaunay skeleton with O(D) exceptional vertices. The quadratic
old isolate population is retained. Outside that skeleton the triangles
have the stated local near-equilateral control and interior valence six.

This is a transfer for bounded closed unit faces, not nonunit-chord
outer pockets or exterior caps. The completion is not yet a packet, and
O(D) defect currency cannot be dropped from an exact one-integer argument.
It identifies the remaining load-bearing theorem: move/pay the completed
defects and boundary cost through an exact full-source exchange, or
derive a positive dual supported on this actual skeleton that localizes
a removable guard/diameter cover.

## 5. Next attacks and limits

Three different attacks survive:

- If the added-point branch occurs, use its controlled missing site and
  the boundary curvature profiles in a nonaffine vacancy-opening packet.
- If it does not, propagate or relocate the bounded defect set using
  exact Delaunay/bar constraints, rather than imposing a vacancy chart.
- For boundary-order canonical slack, couple the O(D) skeleton to actual
  wall/core or face-train motion, keeping every boundary and completion
  label in the contact/diameter/cardinality ledger.

The result supplies neither a global crystalline orientation after removing
defects nor finite compatibility between different developed regions.
Defect holonomy and cuts must be proved or paid. No triangle-free globally
optimal family, general chamber contradiction, C-II or Level I closure
is asserted. The continuous closure goal remains active.

See `target_c_one_deficit_diffuse_strain_nonglobal_regression_2026-10-07.md`
for an exact regression against upgrading this alternative to discrete
vacancy localization. Its whole-source square competitor certifies
nonglobality, so it supplies no Level I disproof.
